92186110674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111615 #edb/ref 1061 40731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4bbacca-99b9-57fe-b9c5-a2075ef9663b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1061 9452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "04c2712b-f67a-5317-b34e-8936b6acdd9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1061 9454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f398499-a037-5d65-8f25-ecbcfa7534c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111765 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1061 9459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14f9869d-2b87-58dd-a3bd-3dfd6047425b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1061 9460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "615b803e-ca7c-56d1-9167-9829ed84b5c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1064 "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](images/94/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1065 2262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06a11fa2-5911-52f5-9a6d-1028e49b44dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111845 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1064 "We can organize all mentioned types of quadrilaterals into the following hierarchy diagram:\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nEach shape below another is a specific type of the one above it. For example, every square is a rectangle, and every rectangle is a parallelogram. This means every square is also a parallelogram." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1065 5931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d9d3be2f-753b-5a4b-9f26-20b34ab8f89a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111900 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying Quadrilaterals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111906 #edb/ref 1064 "For any two distinct points, there is always one (and only one) line that passes through them.\n\nFor example, the line passing through the points $P$ and $Q$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ},$ while the line that passes through $A$ and $B$ is $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/8c/8c79a551e7ced71de157ea292baa3d7f38afaca4ea76ee467fc4add1b28ed33f.png)\n\nHowever, given three or more points, there is not always a line that passes through all of them.\n\nFor example, for the three points $F, G,$ and $H$ below, it is impossible to draw a single straight line that passes through all of them.\n\n![](images/30/30361c0bd49e571e317746f2d88848626da52e5ddbcda8fe9b6f496e4aff6b69.png)\n\nHowever, it is *sometimes* possible to draw a line through three (or more) points. In such cases, we say that the points are **collinear**.\n\nFor example, the points $M,$ $N,$ and $O$ shown below are collinear since they all lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3de55518c21de30718b2d9fee7032139c89aa3a4a963b8346b457ceafbeada.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111906 #edb/ref 1065 6732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111906 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "051ac95a-dc63-5270-a5fa-a498a36c8297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111906 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186135609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135604 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3671129c-87d8-52b4-8590-501af8b56cbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135605 #edb/ref 1080 "$x < - 2$ or $x \\ge 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135605 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135605 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "87473113-3785-52d6-827e-76990b9db25e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135606 #edb/ref 1080 "-2 < x \\le 2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135606 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135606 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a15cebd2-4270-56ea-88b6-de78ac744d61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135607 #edb/ref 1080 "-2 \\le x \\le 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135607 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135607 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aded3bb4-d0d0-5eba-8861-518d2a88fecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135608 #edb/ref 1080 "-2 \\le x < 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135608 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135608 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21fb414b-59f2-54e2-9a8f-e74e2382601e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135609 #edb/ref 1080 "-2 < x < 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135609 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135609 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "579bd95b-c69c-51a8-8b9e-ec25a0a3699a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138536 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 1759218605